BUG cursor jumps into the prompt on the first readline redraw after a kill-server + fresh start (restore path). The cursor starts in the right spot; pressing C-p or C-n then draws the recalled command over the prompt, with the cursor at the right offset into that misplaced text -- i.e. bash and libghostty disagree about the column. Not reproduced yet. Ruled out with the real prompt.bash PS1, driving the real binary through a pty client: reload and restore at same/smaller/larger attach sizes; plain resize and detach+reattach; prompt lengths straddling the wrap boundary (78-81 at 80 cols); prompt on the bottom row with a wrapping recall; wrapped-to-unwrapped widening (100->200 with a 150-col prompt); pane vs shell winsize after restore (stty agrees). Character width is not it: glibc wcwidth and libghostty both give 1 for the powerline U+E0A0, and the outer terminal is ghostty, same tables. hat renders the outer cursor with an absolute CUP straight from the emulator snapshot (View.paneCursor), so a wrong column is a real bash/emulator disagreement, not a render offset. Separate upstream quirk found while hunting, NOT this bug: readline miscounts by the byte length of a TRAILING invisible escape group in PS1 when a wrapped prompt unwraps on a widening resize -- byte-identical under tmux and on a bare pty, and prompt.bash ends with a visible space so it does not fire. NEXT: capture the pane bytes live -- hat pipe-pane -t <pane> "cat >> /tmp/pane.log" right after reattaching, then C-p/C-n; the emitted stream says whether bash mispositions itself or hat does. | id:aff36b02c37016e1feb1bf558a02f410079aa42a
